My email address is there.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:08.890
Peter.Cooper@NIH.gov.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.629
I will talk about what I mean by genomic context.

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:22.590
Then we'll talk about the identical proteins
group and how to see the corresponding genomic

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.929
proteins for BLAST hits.

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:26.869
We'll use the graphics links on BLAST.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:31.660
At the end we will wrap it up by showing you
some is scripting ways to get to some of this

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.570
information using EDirect and some additional
programs.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.579
What do I mean by genomic context?

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:45.809
I mean what genes or protein products or regulatory
sequences or other features are either upstream

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:48.590
or downstream in the genome for matched proteins.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.820
These are the kind of things that everybody
is interested in because he can tell you about

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.059
regulatory mechanisms.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:01.420
It's useful to know from a comparative genomics
perspective how does this set of genes change

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.570
over times.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.580
Are there multiple copies?

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.940
Those kinds of things.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:16.900
Also, another sort of way to use this is a
quality check on genome assemblies and a annotation.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.960
This genome is missing, this particular gene,
maybe that is a problem with the assembly?

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.060
You could look at things like that.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:34.290
So the particular set of genes that we will
look at today are the ones involved in the

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:41.880
type III restriction endonuclease cassette.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.550
To get rid of foreign DNA.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.340
This is a typical example of what you see
if you look into a bacterial genome.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.290
You will see there is helicase at the 5-prime
end of this thing.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:01.950
There is methyl transfer here which is the
thing that methyl lates bacterial genomic

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.500
DNA of the host so that it does not get cut.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.470
And then there is a binding protein.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:14.879
This is a type III restriction endonuclease
subunit R. That is a typical situation.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:20.560
Look around and there are some changes in
this in some kinds of bacteria.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.470
This is the canonical one that we will be
looking at today.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:30.590
What we will do is focus on the site specific
DNA methyl transferase.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.020
Will look for that.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.220
Find it in BLAST and take a look at the genome
and see if the other two genes are flanking

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.220
it.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.780
I hope that makes some sense.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:47.770
So if we are doing a nucleotide search, this
is actually a straightforward thing to do.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:52.710
What I mean by nucleotide search is, I mean
the database is nucleotide.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.909
We are looking down in the alignment section.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:02.099
When we have a tblast in search like this
one, where I search with a protein, and I

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:06.219
match the protein translation of a genomic
sequence, I can click the graphics link and

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:11.680
it will take me directly to a view of that
genomic sequence in our graphical sequence

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.319
viewer.

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:14.860
Like this one.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.930
And there is my protein match.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.950
There is a number of mismatches they are you
can see the bottom of the screen here.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.120
The red marks are mis matches.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.719
That is still a very good protein blast match.

00:03:27.719 --> 00:03:33.540
A tblastn alignment against the subject sequence
which is the whole genomes shotgun sequence

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.420
from this bacterium.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:43.120
If you check the annotation of these genes
on either side, they are similar to the ones

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.739
that I showed you a minute ago.

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:49.519
One is labeled a hypothetical protein, if
you take that in your BLAST search you will

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:51.890
see that it matches one of the other two things.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:58.080
This site-specific transferase is annotated
in the right way.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.080
So the genomic context in this case is conserved.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:07.480
We saw it simply by clicking one link on the
BLAST output.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.450
What a lot of people do when they do these
searches is a protein search.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:21.110
In part to do a tblastn search against large
genomes is burdensome, and they fail for various

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.180
reasons.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.140
Protein is more likely to work.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.010
The problem is the graphics link does not
do what you think it might.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:36.100
It takes you to the protein sequence graphic
which doesn't tell you about the genomic context.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:42.530
However, there is another link that is often
shown on the right-hand side when there is

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:47.070
more than one protein available, the identical
proteins.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:52.490
This is a particular kind of protein accession
number that short that starts with a WP.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.500
This is a nonredundant, RefSeq bacterial proteins.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.400
I will talk more about that in a minute.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:07.152
So if I link to the protein itself, in our
entrez system web database, I can see a link

00:05:07.152 --> 00:05:09.290
to identical proteins there.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:16.240
I can follow the link directly to the IPG
report and this is a list of all the proteins

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.260
that are identical.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:26.060
There may be several GenPept submitted sequences
identical to this RefSeq.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:33.530
But the WP is annotated on a number of different
genomic sequences.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:38.010
So you can see those in the second column
of the table on the right-hand side of this

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.010
slide.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:44.510
If I followed the link to those, it will take
me to the coding region in the nucleotide

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:51.230
database with that particular range and that
particular strand of the DNA focused in on.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:54.590
That will give me a way of looking at the
corresponding coding regions in all of these

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.870
bacteria that have these protein annotated
on it.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:06.830
For example, this is the protein link from
the protein will be looking at the genome.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.410
This is a salmonella enterica assembly.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:18.540
I am just looking at the 3000 bp corresponds
to the coding region of the BLAST match.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.330
If you want to try to look at the view link
is there in the slide.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:23.330
www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/NZ_AZKP01000022.1?from=149913&amp;to=153038

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:32.760
If I follow this to the genomic view, I can
manipulate the graphics view a little bit,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:38.780
and I can take a look at the things surrounding
that particular coding region.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.840
So that is the one that we were zoomed in
on a minute ago.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.310
That is labeled as a site specific DNA methyl
transferase, what I expect my blast search

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.450
to match.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:55.170
We are not going directly from blast so it
doesn't show you the alignment.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.950
We were just aligned protein to protein.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:03.420
I am looking at the region on the genome that
corresponds to that protein.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.980
Upstream of that is the ATP -dependent helicase
that we saw the beginning when talking about

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:12.790
the canonical version of this.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:18.840
And on the other side is the type III restriction
protein binding site.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.710
Subunit.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:27.400
We saw that by following the links to the
identical protein report and then to the graphical

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.630
sequence viewer.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.260
So that's how you do it on the web.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.780
I will demonstrate that live in a few minutes.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:41.170
I wanted to mention that you can access these
identical protein group reports using the

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:46.580
API in particular we will focus on using EDirect
to do that.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:52.910
And we have a number of webinars on EDirect
if you want.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.500
You can go to the link on the slide for YouTube
playlist.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.742
EDirect is a UNIX command line version of
Entrez utilities.

00:07:59.742 --> 00:08:04.260
The API for the Entrez system.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.970
Lets you search and retrieve records from
all of our databases.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:14.880
Has processing scripts and in XML parser,
called xtract.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:23.170
Each program outputs XML and you can pipe
it into another EDirect program or a linux,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:24.860
Unix utility.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:29.090
We talked about this in various webinars,
i fact one last week.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.090
You can check those out if you want more information.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.280
Take a look at the documentation.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:32.460
Which is very extensive.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.110
It explains a lot about using EDirect.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.229
I won't have time today to talk a lot about
that.

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:43.899
I will point out some things that are useful
that you can do with these particular reports.

00:08:43.899 --> 00:08:53.230
We will be focusing on E fetch and the program
xtract to look at some various things.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:09:01.189
So the -format ipg, the argument to xtract
will give you the identical proteins group

00:09:01.189 --> 00:09:03.430
table that we saw a minute ago.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:09.249
Here's an example of how you can retrieve
that and get that report on the command line.

00:09:09.249 --> 00:09:13.689
The other thing that is going to be useful
to us if you want to get the genomic context

00:09:13.689 --> 00:09:20.589
once we know what sequence we will be looking
at, we want to use a particular kind of XML

00:09:20.589 --> 00:09:22.050
called GBC.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:29.829
A special XML format that has sort of the
wrappers or built-in xtract functions that

00:09:29.829 --> 00:09:33.740
let you parse out features from the feature
table.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:37.279
For example this rather complicated command
line at the bottom.

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:38.279
efetch -db nuccore -id NZ_AZKPo1000022.1 -format
gbc' | \

00:09:38.279 --> 00:09:39.279
xtract -insd CDS INSDInterval_from INSDInterval_to
protein_id product

00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:44.269
I am retrieving this record from a nucleotide
database in the format gbc.

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:52.399
I'm passing it through xtract and it is using
this INSD argument to look at the CDS feature.

00:09:52.399 --> 00:09:57.920
Within that feature the are are various qualifiers
that you can out put by giving their names.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.850
I can get the interval, I can get the protein
ID.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:07.329
So we can use that in a very crude way today
to get that information.

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:09.259
